## 5. C Program to RISC-V Assembly

A C program can be compiled into RISC-V assembly using a RISC-V GCC toolchain.

The practical workflow:

1. Write a C program.
2. Compile it using RISC-V GCC.
3. Generate assembly output.
4. Generate an object file.
5. Inspect machine instructions using objdump.

### Example

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int i;
    int sum = 0;
    int n = 10;

    for (i = 1; i <= n; i++)
    {
        sum += i;
    }

    printf("Sum = %d\n", sum);

    return 0;
}
```

### Generate Assembly

```bash
riscv64-unknown-elf-gcc -S -O0 -march=rv64i -mabi=lp64 \
code/sum1ton.c -o assembly/sum1ton.s
```

### Generate Object File

```bash
riscv64-unknown-elf-gcc -c -O0 -march=rv64i -mabi=lp64 \
code/sum1ton.c -o assembly/sum1ton.o
```

### Disassemble

```bash
riscv64-unknown-elf-objdump -d assembly/sum1ton.o \
> disassembly/disassembly.txt
```

